 of the victims of the Great Purge of the late 1930s, with 669,929 people arrested and 376,202 executed.","why":"In Marxist–Leninist theory the kulaks, the better-off peasants, were class enemies of the poor.","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":7,"band":"outcast","name":"Prisoners of the Gulag","term":"lagerya (the camps), zona (the zone)","lbl":"c. 0.7–1% (est.)","w":0.85,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/soviet-union-7.jpg","you":"You were sentenced under Article 58 by a troika, without a trial, and sent to a timber camp in the far north, where your food depends on how much you cut.","kid":"Millions of people were sent to the Gulag, prison camps in cold, faraway places, where they were forced to work very hard.","rise":"Between 1934 and 1953, 20 to 40 per cent of the prisoners were released each year, and good workers could earn early release; Naftaly Frenkel himself went from inmate to camp commander. On release many political prisoners were barred from the big cities and sent into exile in remote regions.","why":"The Gulag, the Main Directorate of Correctional Labour Camps, was set up under the OGPU in 1930 and run by the NKVD from 1934.","people":[]}]}</script>
